## Wake word models

None are shipped. An application supplies all three: the two that turn
audio into features, and the one that knows the phrase.

The ready-made set from openWakeWord
(https://github.com/dscripka/openWakeWord) is offered for
non-commercial use only, because of what went into training it. This
library is MIT or Apache-2.0, so shipping those files would take away
a freedom the licence promises. The first two are arguably not covered
by that condition, since one is a fixed sound transform and the other
is Google's, but the wording does not separate them, and a licence
read loosely is a licence read wrong.

Nothing is lost by leaving them out. The models are loaded from a path
either way, and an application already has to supply the one for its
own phrase.

## Code that travels in the built library

These are compiled in, so anything built from this repository carries
them. Their own licence text ships with each crate.

| Crate       | Licence            | What it does           |
|-------------|--------------------|------------------------|
| `cpal`      | Apache-2.0         | Reaching the devices   |
| `symphonia` | MPL-2.0            | Reading sound files    |
| `rubato`    | MIT OR Apache-2.0  | Changing sample rate   |
| `ort`       | MIT OR Apache-2.0  | Running the model      |
| `thiserror` | MIT OR Apache-2.0  | Error types            |
| `pyo3`      | MIT OR Apache-2.0  | The Python binding     |

Two of these ask for something when a built library is handed on.

**cpal**, under the Apache License 2.0, asks that the licence travel
with what is distributed and that changes to its files be marked. This
project makes no changes to it. It carries no NOTICE file, so there is
none to pass along.

**symphonia**, under the Mozilla Public License 2.0, asks that anyone
given a built copy be told how to get the source of the MPL-covered
files. That source is at https://github.com/pdeljanov/Symphonia. The
licence covers those files alone; it places no condition on the rest of
this project.

Nothing here reaches a network, at build time or after. The only
exception is building `ort`, which fetches the ONNX Runtime binaries it
links against.
